    {
      "i": 4,
      "beat": "bridge",
      "narration": "You've seen the slope at one point. But what if we wanted the slope at any x, not just 0? Let's say f of x is a to the x again. I'd write the difference quotient as f of x plus delta x minus f of x, over delta x, which here is a to the x plus delta x minus a to the x, over delta x. I'll swap delta x for h, kind of a shorthand. The limit of that as h approaches 0 is f prime of x, and that prime mark is Lagrange's notation. Now factor out a to the x, since both terms have it. You get a to the x times the quantity a to the h minus 1, all over h. Sort of interesting, I think. We'll dig into it next.",
      "shots": [
        {
          "phrase": "You've seen the slope at one point.",
          "template": "plot",
          "props": {
            "fn": "Math.pow(2,x)",
            "x_range": [
              -2,
              3
            ],
            "y_range": [
              0,
              8
            ],
            "label": "slope at x=0",
            "point_at": 0,
            "tangent_at": 0,
            "draw": true
          },
          "why": "Anchor on the single point at x=0",
          "start_s": 0.0,
          "end_s": 2.56
        },
        {
          "phrase": "But what if we wanted the slope at any x, not just 0?",
          "template": "plot",
          "props": {
            "fn": "Math.pow(2,x)",
            "x_range": [
              -2,
              3
            ],
            "y_range": [
              0,
              10
            ],
            "label": "slope 1.39",
            "tangent_at": 1
          },
          "why": "Slide the point and tangent away from 0",
          "start_s": 2.56,
          "end_s": 6.83
        },
        {
          "phrase": "Let's say f of x is a to the x again.",
          "template": "plot",
          "props": {
            "fn": "Math.pow(2,x)",
            "x_range": [
              -2,
              3
            ],
            "y_range": [
              0,
              8
            ],
            "label": "f(x) = a^x",
            "point_at": 2,
            "tangent_at": 2
          },
          "why": "Same curve, named a^x, tangent moves on",
          "start_s": 6.83,
          "end_s": 10.33
        },
        {
          "phrase": "I'd write the difference quotient as f of x plus delta x minus f of x, over delta x,",
          "template": "equation",
          "props": {
            "tex": "\\frac{f(x+\\Delta x)-f(x)}{\\Delta x}",
            "highlight": "\\Delta x"
          },
          "why": "Show the general difference quotient",
          "start_s": 10.33,
          "end_s": 17.16
        },
        {
          "phrase": "which here is a to the x plus delta x minus a to the x, over delta x.",
          "template": "equation",
          "props": {
            "tex": "\\frac{a^{x+\\Delta x}-a^{x}}{\\Delta x}",
            "highlight": "a^{x+\\Delta x}"
          },
          "why": "Substitute a^x for f",
          "start_s": 17.16,
          "end_s": 22.99
        },
        {
          "phrase": "I'll swap delta x for h, kind of a shorthand.",
          "template": "equation_morph",
          "props": {
            "from_tex": "\\Large \\frac{a^{x+\\Delta x}-a^{x}}{\\Delta x}",
            "to_tex": "\\Large \\frac{a^{x+\\textcolor{blue}{h}}-a^{x}}{\\textcolor{blue}{h}}"
          },
          "why": "\u0394x visibly becomes h",
          "start_s": 22.99,
          "end_s": 27.09
        },
        {
          "phrase": "The limit of that as h approaches 0 is f prime of x, and that prime mark is Lagrange's notation.",
          "template": "equation_morph",
          "props": {
            "from_tex": "\\frac{a^{x+h}-a^{x}}{h}",
            "to_tex": "f'(x)=\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{a^{x+h}-a^{x}}{h}",
            "highlight": "f'(x)"
          },
          "why": "Add the limit and f\u2032(x)",
          "start_s": 27.09,
          "end_s": 35.11
        },
        {
          "phrase": "Now factor out a to the x, since both terms have it.",
